%I A397806 #52 Sep 09 2026 16:14:58
%S A397806 1,1,2,5,18,66,306,1423
%N A397806 Maximum number of lists of n distinct real numbers in [0, 1) that produce the same list when Gaston's algorithm is applied to them.
%C A397806 We define Gaston's algorithm as below (as in A397807).
%C A397806 Let L = [x_1, x_2, ..., x_n] be a list of size n of distinct real numbers in [0, 1).
%C A397806 Gaston's algorithm creates a new list containing the elements of L.
%C A397806 First, the first element of L is placed in the resulting list.
%C A397806 Then, every term x_i of L is inserted one by one in the resulting list in position k, such as (k - 1)/i <= x_i < k/i.
%C A397806 The list obtained by applying Gaston's algorithm to the list L will be denoted G(L).
%C A397806 a(n) is the maximum number of lists giving the same list (among the n-sized lists) when applying Gaston's algorithm.
%C A397806 Gaston's algorithm was introduced in the eighth problem of the 2026 edition of the TFJM^2 (French Tournament of Young Mathematicians) (see links).
%C A397806 Since the lists giving a list L are always permutations of L, a(n) <= n!.
%C A397806 Furthermore, we can prove that the quotients a(n)/(n!) decrease. Let L = [x_1, ..., x_n] be a list of size n. Given a permutation of L, when the algorithm inserts the last element (which we will denote by x_i) of the permutation, the resulting list cannot be L if the terms of the permutation that have already been inserted are not in the order they are in L. However, considering the list L' with the same elements of L, in the same order but where a_i has been removed, we know that the number of lists giving L' is at most a(n - 1). Consequently, if we set the last element of the permutations of L, at most a(n - 1) of them will give L. Since there are n possible choices for the element x_i in the last position of the permutations, at most n * a(n - 1) permutations of L can give L when applying Gaston's algorithm. Thus, n * a(n - 1) >= a(n) and a(n - 1)/(n - 1)! >= a(n)/(n!).
%C A397806 The following conjectures can be made:
%C A397806 - the maximum number of lists giving the same list L is reached for a sorted list L,
%C A397806 - the maximum number of lists giving the same list L is reached for a sorted list L without duplicates,
%C A397806 - the maximum number of lists giving the same list L is reached for a sorted list L without duplicates and where the i-th element x_i (with an offset of 1) satisfies (i - 1) / n <= x_i < i / n.
%C A397806 Assuming the third conjecture is true, the next two terms would be 7935 and 47181, that are respectively achieved for the lists [0.1, 0.23, 0.3, 0.45, 0.55, 0.65, 0.77, 0.9] and [0.1, 0.18, 0.26, 0.38, 0.48, 0.58, 0.7, 0.78, 0.9].
%H A397806 Tournoi Français des Jeunes Mathématiciennes et Mathématiciens, <a href="https://tfjm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/index.pdf">Problèmes du 16ème Tournoi Français des Jeunes Mathématiciennes et Mathématiciens</a>, version 2, Jan 08 2026, p. 10.
%H A397806 Romain Malécot, <a href="/A397806/a397806.py.txt">Program in Python for a(n), 0 <= n <= 6</a>
%e A397806 Gaston's algorithm is applied as below to the list [0.9, 0.3, 0.6]:
%e A397806 - the first element is inserted in an empty list: [0.9],
%e A397806 - since 0.3 < 1/2, 0.3 is inserted in the first position: [0.3, 0.9],
%e A397806 - since 1/3 <= 0.6 < 2/3, 0.6 is inserted in the second position: [0.3, 0.6, 0.9],
%e A397806 - therefore G([0.9, 0.3, 0.6]) = [0.3, 0.6, 0.9].
%e A397806 For n = 3, let us list the permutations of the list [0.3, 0.6, 0.9] and apply the Gaston's algorithm:
%e A397806   L_1 = [0.3, 0.6, 0.9] -> [0.3, 0.6, 0.9] = L_1,
%e A397806   L_2 = [0.3, 0.9, 0.6] -> [0.3, 0.6, 0.9] = L_1,
%e A397806   L_3 = [0.6, 0.3, 0.9] -> [0.3, 0.6, 0.9] = L_1,
%e A397806   L_4 = [0.6, 0.9, 0.3] -> [0.3, 0.6, 0.9] = L_1,
%e A397806   L_5 = [0.9, 0.3, 0.6] -> [0.3, 0.6, 0.9] = L_1,
%e A397806   L_6 = [0.9, 0.6, 0.3] -> [0.3, 0.9, 0.6] = L_2.
%e A397806 So on one hand, a(3) >= 5, because 5 lists give L_1.
%e A397806 On the other hand, let L = [x_1, x_2, x_3] be a list of size 3. So that G(L) = L, x_2 must be greater than or equal to 0.5. Nevertheless, so that G([x_3, x_2, x_1]) = L, x_2 must be smaller than 0.5. Consequently, it is not possible to create a list of size 3 that would result from applying Gaston's algorithm to more than 3! - 1 = 5 lists; i.e., a(3) <= 5.
%e A397806 Hence, a(3) = 5.
%o A397806 (Python) # See Links.
